/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490184 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490184 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490184 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d17d82c6-1a23-5c4e-bb97-55ab46a58407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3250f349-fbfe-56f8-96e2-52cb212003b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490232 #edb/ref 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f93db21-c74e-5e95-a404-6814f4df05d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490239 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1071 "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60940cd3-550d-521c-8bf6-1906baac5be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490257 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/6b/6b2da4bfc7c768788d143999fb61e76bb3a60facffc35e430d98085b395932f7.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d3bda62-1eef-5cb9-886b-004877c9a204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490279 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5b958d4-e62e-522f-9fd3-554270dc7fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490285 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-54293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a635937b-674b-54dd-a770-48fe7b870be8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490287 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c33968d9b5c86e39ba35c88a5f28bb8950178004c763a5c011633b9a060e98a8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acf0d12e-cf58-5db7-b66f-2999c47f2d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490291 #edb/ref 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
